{"name":"Pandora’s Box","hex":"#fedbb7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pandora","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any fish of the genus Pagellus."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pandora"},{"term":"box","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Buamu."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Senses relating to a three-dimensional object or space.\n A cuboid space; a cuboid container, often with a hinged lid.","A cuboid space; a cuboid container, often with a hinged lid."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/box"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pandora's box","blocks":[{"text":"Pandora's box is an artefact in Greek mythology connected with the myth of Pandora in Hesiod's c. 700 B.C. poem Works and Days. Hesiod related that curiosity led her to open a container left in the care of her husband, thus releasing various deadly evils upon mankind, such as brutal toil and terrible illnesses. Later depictions of the story have been varied, with some literary and artistic treatments focusing more on the contents than on Pandora herself."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Lawrence_Alma-Tadema_10.jpeg/330px-Lawrence_Alma-Tadema_10.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pandora's_box","wikidata":"Q937164"},{"title":"Box","blocks":[{"text":"A box is a container with rigid sides used for the storage or transportation of its contents. Most boxes have flat, parallel, rectangular sides. Boxes can be very small or very large and can be used for a variety of purposes, from functional to decorative."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/L%C3%A5da_-_Livrustkammaren_-_107142.tif/lossy-page1-330px-L%C3%A5da_-_Livrustkammaren_-_107142.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Box","wikidata":"Q188075"},{"title":"Pandora","blocks":[{"text":"In Greek mythology, Pandora was the first human woman, created by Hephaestus on the instructions of Zeus. As Hesiod related it, each deity cooperated by giving her unique gifts. Her other name—inscribed against her figure on a white-ground kylix in the British Museum—is Anesidora."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Abel-josef-1764-1818-austria-prometheus-merkur-und-die-pand.jpg/330px-Abel-josef-1764-1818-austria-prometheus-merkur-und-die-pand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pandora","wikidata":"Q131346"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Pithos_Louvre_CA4523.jpg/500px-Pithos_Louvre_CA4523.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Pithos_Louvre_CA4523.jpg/1280px-Pithos_Louvre_CA4523.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Pithos_Louvre_CA4523.jpg/500px-Pithos_Louvre_CA4523.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A pithos from Crete, c. 675 BC. Louvre","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APithos_Louvre_CA4523.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Wedding_procession_pyxis_BM_GR1920.12-21.1_by_Marley_Painter.jpg/500px-Wedding_procession_pyxis_BM_GR1920.12-21.1_by_Marley_Painter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Wedding_procession_pyxis_BM_GR1920.12-21.1_by_Marley_Painter.jpg/1280px-Wedding_procession_pyxis_BM_GR1920.12-21.1_by_Marley_Painter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Wedding_procession_p